{"sql": "
-- So player 40 was striker in 129 out of 130 matches. That makes sense - they might have 
// been non-striker when they got out, or there could be edge cases.

// Actually, earlier my query showed 21 matches for player 40. Let me re-examine.
// The issue was that I was joining player_match with ball_by_ball, and the GROUP BY was
// grouping by player_id and match_id, but only for matches where there was at least one ball
// where the player was striker. So matches where the player was in player_match but 
// never striker would be excluded.

// So the correct approach is: use player_match as the base, and LEFT JOIN to get runs.

// Let me verify my latest query approach is correct:
WITH player_match_runs AS (
    SELECT 
        pm.player_id,
        pm.match_id,
        COALESCE(SUM(bs.runs_scored), 0) AS total_runs_in_match
    FROM player_match pm
    LEFT JOIN ball_by_ball bb 
        ON pm.match_id = bb.match_id
        AND pm.player_id = bb.striker
    LEFT JOIN batsman_scored bs 
        ON bb.match_id = bs.match_id
        AND bb.over_id = bs.over_id
        AND bb.ball_id = bs.ball_id
        AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP BY pm.player_id, pm.match_id
)
SELECT player_id, match_id, total_runs_in_match
FROM player_match_runs
WHERE player_id = 40
ORDER BY total_runs_in_match DESC
LIMIT 10;
", "preview_rows": 15}

